Description:
 ros-jazzy-legged-rl-controllers - Model of legged robot
Changes:
 ros-jazzy-legged-rl-controllers (3.4.1-2026.08.19.08.10) noble; urgency=high
 .
   * Validate policy metadata and refuse to write non-finite commands
     The controller trusted the ONNX metadata and the policy output without
     checking either, so a malformed export or a NaN action reached the
     hardware silently.
     - Cross-check default_joint_pos, joint_stiffness and joint_damping
     against joint_names in on_activate. These are indexed per joint and
     command_interfaces_ is claimed as 5 * joint_names.size(), so a
     mismatch was an out-of-bounds write from the update loop, which glibc
     reports as "malloc(): corrupted top size".
     - Clamp the update loop to the smallest of the joint count, the action
     size and action_scale. Nothing ties the ONNX action dimension to
     joint_names, in either direction: a policy may emit more than one term
     per joint, and a short action_scale or action vector would otherwise
     be read past its end. That read yields finite garbage, which the
     finiteness check below cannot see.
     - Take currentPosition from the model's joint count. It is indexed by an
     index into getJointNames(), not by an action index.
     - Reject an observation_history_lengths list whose length does not match
     the number of observation terms, or that contains a zero. A zero
     leaves the history deque empty but marked initialised, so the next
     getValue() pops an empty deque.
     - Catch std::exception around parseCsv: std::stoul throws
     std::invalid_argument, a logic_error, which escaped the old handler.
     - Refuse to write a non-finite command. ros2_control stores the value
     unchecked, and a joint limiter does not help either since clamping
     leaves NaN unchanged. Note this is not a skip-one-cycle path: ERROR
     makes controller_manager deactivate the controller and run a command
     mode switch, whose stop path zeroes kp on every joint and leaves only
     damping, so the robot goes limp and settles. That is much better than
     writing NaN while kp is 350, but it is an e-stop. Publish policy_io
     before the check so the record explaining the command survives.
     - Return const references from the Policy getters and hoist
     getDefaultJointPositions out of the per-joint loop, which was
     allocating and freeing once per joint per control cycle.
     - Call the base ObservationTerm::reset from PhaseObservationTerm::reset.
     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
   * Contributors: qiayuanl
